  A polynomial is an exhaustive set of equivalent polynomial expressions.
  A polynomial expression is an arithmetical expression
  mixing constants and variables.

  //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polynomial

  An arithmetical expression is a mathematical expression
  involving only addition and multiplication (sums and products).

  //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elementary_arithmetic

  An addition is sometimes called a subtraction (difference) ——
  —— usually when involving one or several negative numbers.
  A multiplication is sometimes called a division (ratio) ——
  —— usually when involving one or several rational numbers.
  The notation of a repeated multiplication of the same term by itself
  can be simplified with exponentiation of said term
  to a positive integer power.

  Because of this property,
  classical definitions often present exponentiation
  as a third type of allowed operation.
  This is valid for polynomials
  as long as the exponents are positive integers.
  That is:
  a polynomial can feature exponentiation
  if and only if
  said exponentiation is in fact a product.

  Choose the less confusing formulation of the definition.
  I like mine better (hopefully), but perhaps I'm weird.

  A polynomial can be defined by several distinct (but equal) expressions.
  One of them is called canonical (or standard).
  It's obtained by making use of
  commutativity, associativity and distributivity
  /*
    //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commutative_property
    //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Associative_property
    //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Distributive_property
  */
  so that each instance of a variable
  (or product of variables if more than one)
  with the same exponent(s) appears only once,
  therefore having a single constant factor.

  E.g.:  ax² + bx²  =  (a + b)x²

  This single constant factor is called a coefficient.
  A coefficient's association
  with its corresponding exponentiation of the variable
  forms a term.
  The canonical form is the sum of all terms.
  Each term has a degree, equal to its exponent.
  The *degree*, or *order*, of the polynomial
  is the highest degree in the canonical form.
  The term of highest degree is called the leading term.
  Its coefficient is called the *leading coefficient*.
  Any coefficient can be zero
  **except** the leading coefficient.
  If the leading coefficient is 1,
  the polynomial is called a *monic* polynomial.
  The coefficient associated with the zeroth power of the variable
  is called the *constant term*.

  /*
    Note that,
    since there's a zeroth term,
    the number of terms is greater than the degree / oder by 1 ——
    —— if we include potential null terms.
    E.g. (1):

        x⁴ + 2  =  (1)x⁴ + (0)x³ + (0)x² + (0)x¹ + (2)x⁰

      degree:  4
       terms:  5

    E.g. (2):
      y = x  is a special case
      of the affine function:  y  =  a₁x¹ + a₀x⁰
      which is a polynomial of order 1
      with coefficients  a₁ = 1  and  a₀ = 0 .
      In this case, a₁ is the leading coefficient,
      a x  is the leading term, a₀ is the constant term.

    E.g. (3):
      y = 1.33  is equivalent to  y  =  1.33 x⁰
      which is a polynomial of order 0
      whose unique coefficient is  a₀ = 1.33 .
      In this case,
      the leading coefficient,
      the leading term,
      and the constant term
      are indistinguishably: a₀.

    And of course:  y  =  a₂x² + a₁x + a₀
    is a polynomial of order 2 ——
    —— as long as a₂ is non-zero.
  */

  The canonical form:    a[N]xᴺ + a[N—1]x⁽ᴺ⁻¹⁾ + ... + a[1]x + a[0]

                        N
  or, equivalently:     ∑ a[n]xⁿ
                       n=0

  can be re-expressed as:    a[N]{ xᴺ + ( a[N—1]/a[N] )x⁽ᴺ⁻¹⁾ + ... + ( a[1]/a[N] )x + a[0]/a[N] }

                          N   a[n]
  or, better yet:    a[N] ∑  —————— xⁿ
                         n=0  a[N]

  This factorisation allows to extract a monic polynomial
  out of any polynomial.
  (And it's valid because: a[N] is non-zero by definition of polynomial.)
  Beware of amphibology:
  this expression is sometimes loosely referred to as factored form ——
  —— but this name is more typically reserved for a distinct form,
  with which we'll deal in a few lines.
  I won't be using this equivocal acception,
  but know that it happens elsewhere.
  Factoring out the leading coefficient to extract a monic
  is quite often called normalising the polynomial.
  Therefore, should we need a name for this form,
  let's agree on "normalised form".

  A polynomial with a single term is called a monomial.
  Two terms? Binomial.
  Three terms? Trinomial.
  &c.
  (E.g.:  x⁷  is a septic monic monomial...)

  A polynomial with strictly real coefficients
  *in canonical form*
  is called a real polynomial.
  (If the canonical form has only real coefficients,
  so does the normalised form.)
  If it has complex coefficients,
  it's a complex polynomial.
  (Real numbers are special cases of complex numbers.
  Real polynomials are special cases of complex polynomials.)
  If it's not in canonical (or normalised) form,
  we cannot say whether it's real.
  Important distinction:
  "real" in "real polynomial"
  only concerns the coefficients ——
  —— the variable can still be complex.

  A polynomial of negative order is called a Laurent polynomial
  (named after Pierre Alphonse Laurent).
  More rigorously:
    a Laurent polynomial can have a positive order;
    its specificity is that it allows negative exponents.

  //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laurent_polynomial
  //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pierre_Alphonse_Laurent

  Since  x⁻ⁿ = 1/xⁿ , a Laurent polynomial is not defined at  x = 0 .
  (We won't be needing Laurent polynomials before Chapter VII.)

  We now see how whether division happens in polynomials
  really depends on perspective:
  textbooks tend to answer "no, never".
  Let's be more rigorous than textbooks:
  the canonical and normalised forms of non-Laurent polynomials
  are not supposed to show division...
  But if I write:
                                                             //
              x/11 — 17/x           1            —17         //  ƒ(x)  is a Laurent polynomial
    ƒ(x)  =  —————————————  =  —————————— x  +  ————— x⁻¹    //  of the first order.
                 15/10          11 · 3/2         3/2         //  Its leading coefficient is:  2/33 .
                                                             //  Its constant term is:  0 .
          =  ( 2/33 )x¹ + 0·x⁰ + ( —34/3 )x⁻¹                //

  /*
    Fun fact:
    a₀ is the only coefficient
    not multiplied by a non-zero power of x.
    So no matter the (non-Laurent) polynomial's order,
    its value at  x = 0  is always a₀.
    ("Fun" is open for debate, but a fact it most certainly is.)
  */

  The values of the variable(s) at which a polynomial outputs zero
  are called its **_R_O_O_T_S_**.

  ƒ(x)  is a polynomial?
  ƒ(r)  =  0 ?
  Then r is a ROOT of  ƒ(x) .
  ƒ(x)  is a polynomial?
  ƒ(x)  =  0  has 17 solutions for x?
  ƒ(x)  has 17 roots.

  (Sometimes, roots are called "zeros" or "zeroes" ——
  —— in the context of filter design,
  this would be confusing.
  I'll stick to "roots".)

  Let's build stuff.

  //  Order 0 "constant"        https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constant_function

    ƒ(x)  =  a₀

  Then  ƒ(x)  =  0  means  a₀ = 0
  which has 1 solution for a₀ but none for x.
  Therefore a polynomial of the zeroth order
  has zero root.

  //  Order 1 "affine" or "linear"        https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linearity#Linear_polynomials

    ƒ(x)  =  a₁x + a₀

    ƒ(x) = 0    <=>    a₁x + a₀  =  0

                <=>    a₁( x + a₀/a₁ )  =  0

                <=>    x  =  —a₀/a₁
  /*
    This normalising factorisation is relevant
    because a₁ is non-zero
    by definition of a polynomial.
  */

  Therefore a polynomial of the first order
  has one root:  r₁ = —a₀/a₁ .
  And:

    a₁( x + a₀/a₁ )  =  a₁( x — r₁ )

  shows that it can be expressed in terms of this root.
  And this is the *factored form*.
  This is very important,
  so just to make sure you don't rush over this tiny line:
  The **factored form**
  is the expression of a polynomial
  **in terms of its roots**.

  //  Order 2 "quadratic"        https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quadratic_formula

    a₂x² + a₁x + a₀  =  0

    <=>    a₂( x² + (a₁/a₂)x + a₀/a₂ )  =  0

  /*
    [blah] a₂ [blah] non-zero [blah] definition &c.
  */

    <=>    x² + (a₁/a₂)x + a₀/a₂  =  0

    <=>    x² + (a₁/a₂)x  =  —a₀/a₂

  Pause.
  Let's consider the general case:

    (α + β)²  =  (α + β)(α + β)  =  αα + αβ + βα + ββ  =  α² + 2αβ + β²

  Now  x² + (a₁/a₂)x  has the form:  α² + 2αβ
  where:

    α = x    and    a₁/a₂  =  2β    so    β  =  a₁/(2a₂)

  Then:    β²  =  a₁²/(2a₂)²

  We can add any term we want to the equation,
  as long as it happens on both sides, so:

    x² + (a₁/a₂)x + a₁²/(2a₂)²  =  a₁²/(2a₂)² — a₀/a₂

    <=>    ( x + a₁/(2a₂) )²  =  a₁²/(2a₂)² — a₀/a₂

                                  a₁² — 2²a₂a₀
                              =  —————————————
                                     (2a₂)²

                               ┌───────────────
                               │  a₁² — 4a₂a₀
    <=>    x + a₁/(2a₂)  =  ±  │ —————————————
                              ╲│     (2a₂)²
                                ____________
                               √ a₁² — 4a₂a₀
                         =  ± ———————————————
                                    2a₂
                     _____________
                    √ a₁² — 4a₂a₀         a₁
    <=>    x  =  ± ————————————————  —  ——————
                          2a₂            2·a₂
                         ____________
                  —a₁ ± √ a₁² — 4a₂a₀
              =  —————————————————————
                          2a₂

  a₁² — 4a₂a₀  is called the polynomial's discriminant.
  (Hence "discriminating" meaning "finding the roots".)
  It's usually notated Δ (upper—case delta).

  /*
    My notation is not standard
    (well it both is and is not,
    but you'll see that I'm not the inconsistent one here).
    Expressing polynomials' coefficients with indexes of "a" or "b" is standard
    (the latter usually being reserved for numerators, the former for denominators).

    But when dealing with the special cases of affine or quadratic,
    the classical notation is (respectively):

      ax + b    and    ax² + bx + c

    I don't like this.
    You get  a₁ = a , then  a₁ = b , &c.
    It's confusing for no reason.
    Plus it complies poorly with generalisation:
    if you keep raising the order you'll get  a₀ = d  (as in  dƒ/dx ...),
    then  a₀ = e  (as in  exp(1) ...),
    then  a₀ = f  (as in  ƒ(x)  or  2πf ...);
    orders 8 and 9 will give i and j (as in  √(—1) ...);
    order 23 gives  a₀ = x ...
    Order 26 starts using Greek letters.
    At some point you'll run out of alphabets.
    You'll never run out of numbers.
    So pick one letter, give it indexes and stick with it.
    Or, as usual, go play with fire.

    Anyway,
    don't be surprised if you come across:  Δ = b² — 4ac .
  */

  Therefore a polynomial of the second order
  has two roots:
                   ___                           ___
            —a₁ + √ Δ                     —a₁ — √ Δ
    r₁  =  ————————————    and    r₂  =  ————————————
                2a₂                           2a₂

  Before moving further on:
  we're still talking about filters —— even though from a distance.
  We might play around with complex numbers
  on our notebooks and GitHub repositories,
  but an analogue voltage, or its digital counterpart,
  needs to remain purely real.
  So I'm assuming real polynomials,
  starting now.
  What comes next might apply to complex polynomials,
  but I haven't checked.
  And I don't want to write something false
  because of too general phrasing.
  In short:
  from now on:    Im(a₂) = Im(a₁) = Im(a₀) = 0

  Notice that if Δ is real and negative, √(Δ) is imaginary.
  Then r₁ and r₂ are complex numbers and:

    Re(r₁)  =  Re(r₂)  =  —a₁/(2a₂)
    Im(r₁)  =   √{Δ}/(2a₂)
    Im(r₂)  =  —√{Δ}/(2a₂)  =  —Im(r₁)

  Then r₁ and r₂ are complex conjugates.
  If Δ is real and positive,
  √{Δ} is real and so are r₁ and r₂.

  So:                                              //
    the roots of a real quadratic polynomial       //
    are either both real or complex conjugates.    //  Pedagogy with a hammer.
  even though:                                     //  Patented by a guy named Friedrich.
    the roots of a real quadratic polynomial       //
    are either both real or complex conjugates.    //
  whereas:                                         //  (Thanks, my friend.
    the roots of a real quadratic polynomial       //  I wish you were here.)
    are either both real or complex conjugates.    //

  Now if  x = r₁ ,  x — r₁  =  0 ,
  and if  x = r₂ ,  x — r₂  =  0 .
  So if x equals r₁ OR r₂:

    (x — r₁)(x — r₂)  =  0    since one of the factors is necessarily zero.

  Then the roots of  (x — r₁)(x — r₂)  are r₁ and r₂.
  Same as  x² + (a₁/a₂)x + a₀/a₂ .

  So, intuitively,
  they're likely equal or proportional.
  Easy to find out:
                                       ___                  ___
                         ╭      —a₁ + √ Δ   ╮ ╭      —a₁ — √ Δ    ╮
    (x — r₁)(x — r₂)  =  │ x — ———————————— │ │ x — ————————————— │
                         ╰          2a₂     ╯ ╰          2a₂      ╯
                      ___            ___                ___           ___
               —a₁ — √ Δ      —a₁ + √ Δ        ( —a₁ + √ Δ  )( —a₁ — √ Δ  )
    =  x² — x ———————————— — ———————————— x + ——————————————————————————————
                   2a₂            2a₂                     (2a₂)²
                    ___         ___                ___      ___    ___
             —a₁ — √ Δ  — a₁ + √ Δ        a₁² + a₁√ Δ  — a₁√ Δ  — √ Δ²
    =  x² — ———————————————————————— x + ——————————————————————————————
                       2a₂                            4a₂²

             —2a₁       a₁² — Δ
    =  x² — —————— x + —————————
              2a₂         4a₂²

                        a₁² — a₁² + 4a₂a₀     //  a₁² — a₁²  cancels out,
    =  x² + (a₁/a₂)x + ———————————————————    //  then  4 a₂  becomes a common factor
                              4a₂a₂           //  to both the numerator and the denominator.

    =  x² + (a₁/a₂)x + a₀/a₂

  Nice.
  Then  a₂( x² + (a₁/a₂)x + a₀/a₂ )  =  a₂(x — r₁)(x — r₂) .
  Therefore any second order (or quadratic) real polynomial
  can be expressed in terms of its roots and leading coefficient.
  Der Hammer redet noch ein Mal:
  this is the factored form of the quadratic.

  Notice that:
                            ___           ___
    a₂(x — r₁)(x — r₂)  =  √ a₂ (x — r₁) √ a₂ (x — r₂)
             ___                 ___
  and that  √ a₂ (x — r₁)  and  √ a₂ (x — r₂)
  are two first—order polynomials
  whose respective roots are r₁ and r₂.

  ↓↓↓  ACHTUNG: FUNDAMENTAL CONCEPT AHEAD  ↓↓↓

  Therefore any second-order polynomial
  is the product of two first-order polynomials,
  whose respective roots
  are also the roots of said second-order polynomial.
  If the second-order polynomial is real,
  the first-order ones are either both real or forming a conjugate pair.
  /*
    Fun example:

      x² — 1  =  0    <=>    x²  =  1
                      ___
      <=>    x  =  ± √ 1   =  ± 1

    So:

      x² — 1  =  (x + 1)(x — 1)

    You can check:

      3² — 1  =  9 — 1  =  8  =  4 · 2
              =  (3 + 1)(3 — 1)

      7² — 1  =  49 — 1  =  48  =  8 · 6
              =  (7 + 1)(7 — 1)

      10² — 1  =  100 — 1  =  99  =  11 · 9
               =  (10 + 1)(10 — 1)

      0.5² — 1  =  0.25 — 1  =  —0.75  =  1.5 · —0.5
                =  (0.5 + 1)(0.5 — 1)

      ϕ² — 1  =  ϕ( ϕ — 1/ϕ )  =  ϕ( ϕ — (ϕ — 1) )
              =  ϕ( ϕ — ϕ + 1 )  =  ϕ·1  =  ϕ
              =  ϕ²/ϕ  =  ϕ²·( 1/ϕ )
              =  (ϕ + 1)(ϕ — 1)

      //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_ratio

    Cool. Cool cool cool.
  */

  Let's assume that r₁ and r₂ are complex and define:
               ___               ___
    P₁(x)  =  √ a₂ (x — r₁)  =  √ a₂ { x — Re(r₁) — j·Im(r₁) }
               ___      ___               ___
           =  √ a₂·x — √ a₂·Re(r₁) — j·√ a₂·Im(r₁)
               ___               ___
    P₂(x)  =  √ a₂·(x — r₂)  =  √ a₂·{ x — Re(r₂) — j·Im(r₂) }
               ___      ___             ___
           =  √ a₂·x — √ a₂·Re(r₂) — j·√ a₂·Im(r₂)

  Then:
                        ___      ___             ___
    P₁(x)·P₂(x)  =   { √ a₂·x — √ a₂·Re(r₁) — j·√ a₂·Im(r₁) }
                        ___      ___             ___
                   · { √ a₂·x — √ a₂·Re(r₂) — j·√ a₂·Im(r₂) }

                 =  a₂x² — a₂Re(r₂)x — ja₂Im(r₂)x

                    — a₂Re(r₁)x + a₂Re(r₁)Re(r₂) + ja₂Re(r₁)Im(r₂)

                    — ja₂Im(r₁)x + ja₂Im(r₁)Re(r₂) — a₂Im(r₁)Im(r₂)
    α₂  =  a₂

    α₁  =  —a₂{ Re(r₂) + j·Im(r₂) + Re(r₁) + j·Im(r₁) }

    α₀  =  a₂{ Re(r₁)Re(r₂) + j·Re(r₁)Im(r₂) + j·Im(r₁)Re(r₂) — Im(r₁)·Im(r₂) }

    Im(α₁)  =  —a₂{ Im(r₂) + Im(r₁) }

    Im(α₀)  =  a₂{ Re(r₁)Im(r₂) + Im(r₁)Re(r₂) }

  For  P₁(x) P₂(x)  to be a real polynomial,
  we need all three coefficients to be purely real.
  That is:

    Im(α₁)  =  0    <=>    Im(r₂) + Im(r₁)  =  0

                    <=>    Im(r₂)  =  —Im(r₁)

    Im(α₀)  =  0    <=>    Re(r₁)Im(r₂) + Im(r₁)Re(r₂)  =  0

                    <=>    Re(r₁)Im(r₂)  =  —Im(r₁)Re(r₂)

                    <=>    Re(r₁)Im(r₂)  =  Re(r₂)Im(r₂)

                    <=>    Re(r₁)  =  Re(r₂)
  /*
    We got this result after dividing both sides by  Im(r₂) ,
    so it's only valid for non-zero imaginary parts for r₁ and r₂.
  */
  What if  Im(r₂) = 0  then?

                      ╭  x² — Re(r₂)x                ╮
    (P₁·P₂)(x)  =  a₂·│  — Re(r₁)x + Re(r₁)Re(r₂)    │
                      ╰  — jIm(r₁)x + jIm(r₁)Re(r₂)  ╯

    Im( (P₁·P₂)(x) )  =  a₂{ Im(r₁)Re(r₂) — Im(r₁)x }

    Im( (P₁·P₂)(x) )  =  0  ∀ x

    <=>    Im(r₁)Re(r₂) — Im(r₁)x  =  0  ∀ x

    <=>    Im(r₁)Re(r₂)  =  Im(r₁)x  ∀ x

  Re(r₂)  is a constant, x is a variable.
  So the only solution for all x is  Im(r₁) = 0 .

  Ok! We now have an "if and only if"
  (or "iff" for friends and family).
  So, any real second-order polynomial
  can be expressed as a product of first-order polynomials,
  either purely real, or with complex conjugate roots.
  AND, any product of two purely real,
  or having complex conjugate roots,
  first-order polynomials
  is a real second-order polynomial.
  There wouldn't be a chapter VIII
  if not for this tiny little "and only if".
  /*
    What if...

      r₁ = r₂ = 0

    ?

    Then:

      P₁(x)P₂(x)  =  a₂(x — 0)(x — 0)  =  a₂x²

    Since  +0 = —0 , any purely real number is its own complex conjugate.
    So this example satisfies the case  r₂ = conj(r₁) .
    But it's also a special case of  r₁ = r₂ .
    Let's generalise that:

      P₁(x)P₂(x)  =  a₂(x — r₁)(x — r₁)  =  a₂( x² — 2r₁x + r₁² )

                  =  a₂x² + 2a₂r₁x + a₂r₁²

    which is a valid second-order polynomial.
    /*
      Note that r₁ and r₂ cannot be equal and conjugates at the same time,
      unless they're purely real.
    */
    So when we say that  order 2  =>  2 roots ,
    we do carefully not say "distinct" roots...

    Also:
    1st-order polynomials which are not exactly conjugates,
    just because of their leading coefficients,
    can be re-arranged as proper conjugates.
    E.g.:

      8( x — 11 + 6j )·2( x — 11 — 6j )  =  16( x — 11 + 6j )( x — 11 — 6j )

      =  4( x — 11 + 6j )·4( x — 11 — 6j )

    More generally:

      a₁{ x — Re(r) — jIm(r) }·b₁{ x — Re(r) + jIm(r) }
          _____                         _____
      =  √ a₁b₁ { x — Re(r) — jIm(r) }·√ a₁b₁ { x — Re(r) + jIm(r) }
  */

  Before raising the order any further,
  there's a special polynomial
  that tends to pop up in filter design and analysis.
  It's called a biquadratic polynomial.
  (Ever heard of biquad filters? —— If not you will soon.)
