#03  Three theorems (but who's counting?)
===================================================================================================

  Let  P[N](x)  be a polynomial of order N,
  and z any complex number.
  (Whether  P[N](x)  is fully complex
  or purely real is undefined until further notice.)
  Then  x — z  is a 1st-order polynomial.
  So if we use  x — z  as the divisor
  in the polynomial long division
  of  P[N](x)  (the dividend),
  the quotient will be of order  N — 1 .
  Let's call it  Q[N—1](x) .
  And the remainder will be of order:  1 — 1  =  0,
  so it will be a constant.
  Let's call it  R[N—1] .
  So,
  based on what has been established during the previous section:

     P[N](x) — R[N—1]
    —————————————————  =  Q[N—1](x)    <=>    P[N](x)  =  (x — z)Q[N—1](x) + R[N—1]
         (x — z)

  Right now if we keep running the loop,
  we risk missing an opportunity to demonstrate a theorem...
  What kind of weirdo wouldn't want to demonstrate every last theorem on Earth?
  (Answering rhetorical questions is rude.)
  What if...  x = z ?

    P[N](z)  =  (z — z)Q[N—1](z) + R[N—1]  =  0 + R[N—1]

    <=>    R[N—1]  =  P[N](z)

  This is the polynomial remainder theorem.

  //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polynomial_remainder_theorem

  It basically says that
  instead of bothering with polynomial long division,
  when the divisor is of order 1,
  you can get the remainder
  by using the last (zeroth-order) coefficient
  of the divisor
  as the dividend's variable.
  The divisor must be normalised
  by factoring out its leading coefficient:

    ax + b  <——  a( x + b/a )

  then the remainder is  R[N—1]  =  P[N](b/a)

  Now imagine we compute  P[N](z)  and it outputs zero.
  It means that z is a root of  P[N](x)
  (by definition of a root).
  It also means that  R[N—1]  =  0
  (according to the polynomial remainder theorem ——
  —— the one we've just demonstrated).
  See:

    P[N](z)  =  0    <=>    (z — z)Q[N—1](z) + R[N—1]  =  0

                     <=>    0 + R[N—1]  =  0    <=>    R[N—1]  =  0

  What this proves:
  when we divide a non-constant polynomial
  by a monic binomial of the first order
  whose constant term
  is the opposite of any of the roots of the dividend ——
  —— there is no remainder.
  The monic and the quotient are called
  factors of the dividend polynomial.
  This is the reason for the factored form's name.

  //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Factorization_of_polynomials

  Plain English may not offer the clearest formulation:

              N
    P₀(x)  =  ∑ a[n]xⁿ    &&    P₀(r₁)  =  0
             n=0
                                 N—1
    <=>        P₀(x)  =  (x — r₁) ∑ b[n]xⁿ
                                 n=0
  Let's define:
                                N—1
    P₁(x)  =  P₀(x)/(x — r₁)  =  ∑ b[n]xⁿ    &&    P₁(r₂)  =  0
                                n=0
  then:
                      N—2
    P₁(x)  =  (x — r₂) ∑ c[n]xⁿ
                      n=0
                                    N—2
    =>    P₀(x)  =  (x — r₁)(x — r₂) ∑ c[n]xⁿ
                                    n=0
    =>    P₀(r₂)  =  0

  So any root of P₁ is also a root of P₀.
  And, of course,
  r₁ is the root of  x — r₁ ,
  and r₂ is the root of  x — r₂ .
  What this proves:
  All roots of a polynomial's factor
  are roots of said polynomial.
  This is the factor theorem.

  //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Factor_theorem

  If we keep running this loop,
  knowing that the order of the quotient
  is the order of the dividend minus the order of the divisor,
  and that the order of our monic first-order binomial divisor
  is always 1,
  we'll end up with  P_N(x)  being a constant factor ——
  —— let's call it Q.
  So:
                N                 //  where  r[ν]
    P₀(x)  =  Q ∏ ( x — r[ν] )    //  are the roots
               ν=1                //  of  P₀(x).

  What this proves:
    the order / degree of a polynomial
    is equal to the number of its roots.

  Equalities read in both directions.
  So:                                                     //  (TRY NOT MISSING THESE TWO LINES)
    (1)  if a polynomial has N roots, it's of order N;    //  ←
    (2)  if it's of order N, it has N roots.              //  ←

  Q being constant,
  the only solutions to  P₀(x) = 0  are  x = r[ν] .
  So N roots is to be understood as a maximum.
  It's an exact number if we admit duplicates as distinct.
  ("Distinct duplicates" is very oxymoronic,
  but it's the only way to characterise
  every possible polynomial in a general statement.)
  E.g.:

    ƒ(x)  =  (x — 11)(x — 11)(x — 11)/2

          =  { (x² — 11x — 11x + 11²)(x — 11)/2 }·(x³ — 22x² + 121x — 11x² + 11·22x — 11³)/2

          =  (x³ — 33x² + 363x — 1331)/2  =  0.5x³ + —16.5x² + 181.5x + —665.5

  The only solution to  ƒ(x) = 0  is  x = 11 .
  So  ƒ(x)  is a third-order polynomial with one root,
  or three equal roots —— as you want.
  Notice that the constant factor
  in the factored form (1/2)
  is equal to the leading coefficient
  in the standard form (0.5).
  Also notice that the last expression of  P₀(x)
  looks very much like a factored form.
  So the next question is:  Q  ==  a[N]  ?
  We got to the final expression
  through successive divisions of  P₀(x)  by  ( x — r[ν] ) .
  We start polynomial long division
  by cancelling the leading term,
  so that the leading term of  P₁(x)  is:  a[N]x⁽ᴺ⁻¹⁾ .
  (Because the divisor's leading term is 1.)
  So as we run the loop,
  the leading coefficient of the quotient
  always remain the leading coefficient ( a[N] )
  of the initial dividend ( P_N(x) ).
  Which means that the leading coefficient of Q is  a[N] .
  Since Q is of order zero,
  its leading coefficient is also its constant term...
  therefore, yep:  Q = a[N]
  THEREFORE:
                                          //  This is the true power of the factor theorem.
                                          //  And it's our "if and only if".
      N                   N               //  Meaning: any product of N 1st-order polynomials
      ∑  a[n] xⁿ  =  a[N] ∏ (x — r[ν])    //  is an Nth order polynomial;
    n = 0                ν=1              //  AND: any Nth order polynomial
                                          //  is a product of N 1st-order polynomials.

  Another way to phrase this conclusion is:
  a polynomial and its non-constant factors
  have the same roots;
  AND the order of a polynomial
  is the number of its non-constant factors.
  A corollary of the factor theorem is:
  every polynomial admits a factored form.

  Ok, next.
  This book is about filters.
  So for these theorems to be of any use,
  we need to know if the factored form
  of a real polynomial
  multiplies real polynomials.
  At first sight: not likely.
  But we don't need only real roots:
  we've seen that pairs of conjugate roots
  form real biquadratic polynomials.
  And since real numbers are their own conjugates
  (their imaginary part is:  0 = —0 ) ——
  —— all we need to check is:
  given any polynomial —— of any order ——
  —— are the conjugates of the roots
  also necessarily roots?
  If yes,
  it would mean that any real polynomial
  can be expressed as a product of real polynomials
  of order 1 and/or 2.

  Let  P[N](x)  be a real polynomial.
  And let's evaluate its value at  z
  (any complex number)
  and at  conj(z)  =  Re(z) — jIm(z) .
  (Remember: "real" polynomial
  means "real coefficients in standard form" ——
  —— the variable can still have an imaginary part,
  hence "z" here.)

               N                                     N
    P[N](z)  = ∑ a[n]zⁿ    <=>    P[N]( conj(z) )  = ∑ a[n]conj(z)ⁿ
              n=0                                   n=0

  Ok, so first,
  let's refresh our complex arithmetic.

    cos  =  adjacent / hypotenuse
     =>    cos( arg(z) )  =  Re(z)/|z|
    <=>    Re(z)  =  |z|cos( arg(z) )

    sin  =  opposite / hypotenuse
     =>    sin( arg(z) )  =  Im(z)/|z|
    <=>    Im(z)  =  |z| sin( arg(z) )

    =>    conj(z)  =  |z|cos( arg(z) ) — j|z|sin( arg(z) )

                   =  |z|{ cos( —arg(z) ) + jsin( —arg(z) ) }

                   =  |z|exp( —j arg(z) )

    =>    |conj(z)|  =  |z|    &&    arg( conj(z) )  =  —arg(z)

    zⁿ  =  |z|ⁿ·exp( j·arg(z) )ⁿ

        =  |z|ⁿ·exp( j n arg(z) )

    =>    |zⁿ|  =  |z|ⁿ    &&    arg(zⁿ)  =  n·arg(z)

    conj(z)ⁿ  =  |z|ⁿ·exp( —j·arg(z) )ⁿ

              =  |z|ⁿ·exp( —j·n·arg(z) )

    conj(zⁿ)  =  |zⁿ|·exp( —j·arg(zⁿ) )

              =  |z|ⁿ·exp( —j·n·arg(z) )

              =  conj(z)ⁿ

  Let's define:  ζ[n] = zⁿ
  Then:

    conj( ζ[n] )  =  conj(zⁿ)  =  conj(z)ⁿ

  So:
               N                                        N
    P[N](z)  = ∑ a[n]ζ[n]    <=>    P[N]( conj(z) )  =  ∑ a[n]conj( ζ[n] )
              n=0                                      n=0

  If  P[N](z)  is a real polynomial,
  then all coefficients  a[n]  are purely real.
  So if:

    Z[n]  =  a[n] ζ[n]

  then:

    Re( Z[n] )  =  a[n]Re( ζ[n] )

  and:

    Im( Z[n] )  =  a[n]Im( ζ[n] )

  and:

    a[n]conj( ζ[n] )  =  a[n]Re( ζ[n] ) — ja[n]Im( ζ[n] )

                      =  Re( Z[n] ) — jIm( Z[n] )  =  conj( Z[n] )
  Then:
               N         N
    P[N](z)  = ∑ Z[n]  = ∑ { Re( Z[n] ) + jIm( Z[n] ) }
              n=0       n=0

               N              N
             = ∑ Re( Z[n] ) + ∑ jIm( Z[n] )
              n=0            n=0

                N                N
             =  ∑ Re( Z[n] ) + j ∑ Im( Z[n] )
               n=0              n=0

  Therefore:
                      N
    Re( P[N](z) )  =  ∑ Re( Z[n] )
                     n=0

  and:
                      N
    Im( P[N](z) )  =  ∑ Im( Z[n] )
                     n=0

  Almost there:
                       N                 N
    P[N]( conj(z) )  = ∑ conj( Z[n] )  = ∑ { Re( Z[n] ) — jIm( Z[n] ) }
                      n=0               n=0

                       N                N
                     = ∑ Re( Z[n] ) — j ∑ —jIm( Z[n] )
                      n=0              n=0
  Therefore:

    P[N]( conj(z) )  =  conj( P[N](z) )

  Ok.
  Now imagine that z is a root of  P[N](x) .
  It means:

    P[N](z)  =  0    =>    P[N]( conj(z) )  =  conj(0)  =  0

  What we've just proven:
  The complex conjugate
  of the root
  of a real polynomial
  is necessarily also a root.
  This is the complex conjugate root theorem.

  //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Complex_conjugate_root_theorem

  A purely real number is its own conjugate,
  so the theorem was obvious in that case.
  But all roots (of a *real* polynomial)
  having an imaginary part
  are guaranteed to come in conjugate pairs.

  Once we express the polynomial
  as a product of first-order polynomials
  and a constant factor,
  assuming each first-order polynomial
  is expressed in terms of its roots,
  these complex conjugate pairs
  will form second-order real polynomials.

  So —— and this is fucking fundamental ——
  —— a real polynomial of any order
  can be expressed
  as a product
  of **real** first-order
  and/or
  second-order polynomials
  and one optional constant factor.
  Ok.



